## 2.3. Catalyst characterization
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained on a Philips PW3040/60 diffractometer, using CuKα radiation $\lambda=0.1541\mathrm{\nm}$ ) in a scanning range of $5{\sim}50^{\circ}$ at a scanning rate of $1^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ .
${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were obtained at $^{-196}\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument. The samples were outgassed under vacuum at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ , prior to the adsorption measurements.
The amount of the Pd in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was measured by an IRIS Intrepid II XSP inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spectrometer (ICP-AES). Because the emission of zirconium in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 is similar to that of the palladium, it is difficult to detect the palladium amount directly by ICP-AES. Instead, we measured the amount of Pd that was washed away during the washing steps and calculated the actual palladium remaining in $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. When loading the 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66, the exact initial weight of the palladium precursor was recorded first. Every time after washing, the washing solution that contained the palladium residue was collected. Finally, ICP-AES was performed for the washing solution, and the palladium content was calculated. The difference between added and removed Pd (during washing steps) is the actual palladium amount that was introduced in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. The same method was also used to determine the actual Pd amount in Pd@UiO-66.
The infrared (IR) spectra were collected on a Nicolet NEXUS670 Fourier transform IR spectrophotometer in KBr disks at room temperature.
The surface electronic states were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo VG ESCALAB250 using AlKα radiation). The XPS data were internally calibrated, fixing the binding energy of C 1s at 284.6 eV.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed on a Hitachi S-4800 apparatus which was performed on a sample powder previously dried and sputter-coated with a thin layer of gold. In addition, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) analyses were also performed to confirm the presence and the distribution of Pd nanoparticles.
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observations were carried out on a JEOL JEM-1200 working at $200~\mathrm{kV}$ . The sample was diluted in ethanol to give a $1:5$ volume ratio and sonicated for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The ethanol slurry was then dropped onto a $\mathrm{Cu}$ grid covered with a thin film of carbon.
Temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) profiles of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ from the MOF catalysts were measured using a Micromeritics AutoChem II 2920 instrument. The sample cell loaded with $0.1\ \mathrm{g}$ of sample was initially treated in a helium flow of $40~\mathrm{mL/min}$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ , then cooled to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and subsequently exposed to pulses of ammonia until saturation and then purged with helium flow at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Afterwards, the sample cell was heated to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a ramp of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ . The concentration of the desorbed ammonia was monitored continuously with a TCD detector. The total amount of the desorbed $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ for the 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd $\ @\operatorname{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst was determined by a reaction with an excess of dilute HCl solution and back titration with NaOH solution, according to the procedure described in our previous work [54].
## 2.4. Hydrodeoxygenation of vanillin
The catalytic hydrodeoxygenation of vanillin was carried out in a $50~\mathrm{ml}$ Teflon-lined autoclave equipped with stirrer, heater, and sample port. In a typical experiment, 2 mmol of vanillin, $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of catalyst, and $20\mathrm{ml}$ water as solvent were charged into the autoclave and were repeatedly purged with hydrogen gas at room temperature. Afterwards, the autoclave was heated at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then pressurized with hydrogen up to $0.5\mathrm{MPa}$ . Finally, the agitation was initiated with a stirring speed of 1000 rpm. A steady pressure was maintained throughout the reaction period. The mixture was extracted with ethyl acetate and the catalyst was taken out from the system by centriDfOuI:g1a0t.1i0o3n9 Cb5eGfCo0r2e615 being analyzed by GC (Agilent 6820) equipped with an FID detector and a capillary column (DB-5, $30\mathrm{m}\times0.45\mathrm{mm}\times0.42\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Additionally, the reaction conditions with respect to temperature, time, hydrogen pressure, and molar ratio of the catalyst amount to vanillin were studied.
In order to test the catalytic reusability of 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd $\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66, after the reaction, the catalyst was separated by filtration, washed with ethanol, and dried in a vacuum desiccator at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ , and then the recovered catalyst was used in the next run reaction.
## 2.5. Hydrogenation of $\pmb{n}$ -hexylene and cyclooctene
The encapsulation of Pd nanoparticles in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was indirectly verified by the hydrogenation of $n$ -hexylene and cyclooctene. Typically, $0.03\ \mathrm{g}$ catalyst, $2.0\:\mathrm{mmol}$ substrate, and 20 mL ethanol were added into a stainless steel autoclave. After replacing the air in the autoclave with argon, 2 MPa $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was injected, and the catalytic tests were then performed at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120\mathrm{min}$ . The products were analyzed by GC (Agilent 6820) with a OV-101 capillary column.
## 2.6. Adsorption Experiments
$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and UiO-66 were dried at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ before adsorption experiments. Adsorption of vanillin was investigated by addition of activated MOFs $(0.05\ \mathrm{g)}$ to the solutions of vanillin $\mathrm{1.0~mmol}\mathrm{\Omega},$ ) in water ( $100~\mathrm{ml}$ ). The mixture was stirred at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The amount of adsorbed vanillin over MOFs was determined by measuring the UV-vis spectra of the solution. The adsorption of vanillin alcohol over $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and UiO-66 was performed with the same procedure to that of vanillin except that vanillin was replaced by vanillin alcohol.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalyst characterization
Pd $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{@}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was prepared by incorporating a Pd precursor into the amine-functionalized metal-organic framework $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 via direct anionic exchange and subsequent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reduction. The prepared catalysts were characterized by means of various techniques. As shown in the XRD patterns in Fig. 1, the encapsulation of Pd into $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 does not influence the crystallinity of the parent
