The FTIR spectrum shows that the characteristic $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretching frequency in the carboxylic acid group of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ linker centered at $2970~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ disappears after formation of the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst, suggesting occurrence of the coordination interaction between ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ ions and carboxylic acid group of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-H_{2}B D C}$ to form $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 (Fig. 3). In addition, two peaks at around 3470 and $3360~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , attributing to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching absorptions of primary amine group, can be clearly discerned over the prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 (curves b and c in Fig.3). These two peaks are down shifted from $3500~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $3400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ respectively in comparison with the pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (curve a in Fig.3), indicating that the amino groups in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 are retained [40]. The presence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group within the framework of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was further analyzed using N1s spectra from the XPS data (Fig. S1 in Supporting Information). The N1s peak for amine group appeared at $400\mathrm{eV}$ .
The loading amount of Pd in (or on) $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 are $1.97\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ and $1.92\ \mathrm{wt.\%}$ , respectively, as determined by the ICP-AES analysis, very close to the nominal amount of $2.0\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ . However, in Pd@UiO-66, only 0.4 wt. $\%$ Pd was detected, when we anticipated to introduce $2.0~\mathrm{wt.}\%$ Pd on the MOF. The majority of Pd precursor for $\operatorname{Pd}\ @$ UiO-66 was lost during the washing steps (probably due to the weak interaction between UiO-66 and the Pd precursor). These results imply that the amine functional groups in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 can strongly favor the loading of metal nanoparticles as reported in other MOFs [42].
From the EDX mapping of a selected area on the 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd $\boldsymbol{@}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst, homogeneous distribution of Pd, Zr and N on the same selected area was observed, suggesting that ultrasmall Pd nanoparticles were effectively encapsulated within the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 MOF (Fig. 4). The dispersion was uniform, and no sign of aggregation was observed. The TEM images of the 2.0 wt. $\%$ $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst, shown in Fig. 5, also confirms the uniformity of the Pd nanoparticles in the matrix of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. The sizes of the Pd nanoparticles were mainly within a range of 1.5 – 2.5 nm in diameter, which are well-dispersed (or embedded) inside the cavities of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 [32]. The slightly larger Pd nanoparticles size than the cage size of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 is probably due to the close contact between Pd nanoparticles with the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ cluster [24]. It should be noted that the increase of the cage size of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 due to defects in the MOF crystals will facilitate the encapsulation of the Pd nanoparticles within the cages [46,47]. Fig. 5d shows that the Pd nanoparticles are crystalline with a spacing of $0.224~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the Pd (111) interplanar spacing [31]. On the other hand, the encapsulation of Pd nanoparticles in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 can also be indirectly verified by the hydrogenation of $n$ -hexylene and cyclooctene [55], in which 2.0 wt. $\%$ P $\mathrm{d}\ @\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 had extremely high activity in the hydrogenation of $n$ -hexylene for its linear structure making it possible to pass through the narrow window of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66. At the same time, the ring reactant (cyclooctene)
