The acidities of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd $\boldsymbol{\ @}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 measured by the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -TPD are shown in Fig. S3. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ -TPD results indicate that the acidity of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 support can be classified into three groups: weak $\mathrm{110-200~^{\circ}C})$ , medium $(210-300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and strong $(330{\-}430\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , very similar to the results reported by Kim et al [50]. Similarly, the $2.0\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ Pd ${\mathsf{I}}@{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst also exhibits the three kinds of acidic sites, although the desorption temperatures of these acidic sites are slightly offset to low temperatures. On the other hand, it should be noted that the medium acidity sites of the 2.0 wt. $\%$ Pd $\boldsymbol{\ @}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst seem to be much higher than those of the unloaded $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 support. Obviously, the major acidity of the 2.0 wt. $\%$ $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 catalyst is originated from the accessible metal $Z\mathbf{r}$ sites having Lewis acidity [48-51], as well as the defects due to the missing of organic linkers between $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ clusters [46,47]. FurthermoDrOeI:, 1a0 1p03o9r/tCi5oGnC0o2f615 the Brønsted acidity can be attributed to the protonation of the amino-functionalized links in the MOF during the synthesis of the $2.0\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ Pd $\boldsymbol{@}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -UiO-66, with the charge being balanced by chloride anions [42,52]. The IR analysis was conducted for the sample after desorption (Fig. S4). It has been reported that the UiO-66 series of MOFs show exceptionally high thermal stability (up to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air) and strong resistance towards water, organic solvents and other chemicals [46,47]. However, compared with parent UiO-66, it should be admitted that thermal stability of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 would decrease to some